# McDonald's Isn't Really a Burger Company

## INTRO — 0:00–0:35

When you think about McDonald's, you probably think about burgers, fries and Happy Meals.

But there's a much bigger business hiding underneath the golden arches.

McDonald's makes money from restaurants, but its business model also revolves around franchising, royalties, rent and real estate.

And once you understand that model, McDonald's starts to look less like a fast-food company and more like a carefully designed business system.

So today, let's break down exactly how McDonald's makes money.

## PART 1 — THE RESTAURANT YOU SEE — 0:35–1:30

Let's start with the obvious part.

A customer walks into a McDonald's restaurant.

They buy a burger.

They buy fries.

Maybe they buy a drink.

The restaurant collects the money.

Simple, right?

But McDonald's doesn't operate every restaurant in exactly the same way.

A major part of the company's strategy is franchising.

Instead of McDonald's providing all the capital and operating every location itself, franchisees can operate restaurants under the McDonald's brand.

The franchisee puts capital into developing and operating the restaurant.

In return, McDonald's receives payments such as royalties and rent, depending on the type of arrangement.

That's where the business model gets interesting.

## PART 2 — THE FRANCHISE ENGINE — 1:30–2:40

Think about what happens when a franchisee operates a McDonald's.

The franchisee is responsible for operating the restaurant.

They need employees.

They need inventory.

They need to serve customers.

But McDonald's owns an incredibly valuable asset:

The brand.

And depending on the arrangement, McDonald's also receives rent and royalties connected to the restaurant.

McDonald's own investor materials describe revenue from rent, royalties and initial fees, as well as profits from restaurants that it operates directly.

That means McDonald's can earn money from the economic activity happening inside restaurants without having to operate every single restaurant itself.

And that's a powerful model.

## PART 3 — WHY FRANCHISING SCALES — 2:40–3:45

Imagine you wanted to open one restaurant yourself.

You would need money.

You would need employees.

You would need equipment.

You would need a location.

You would need to manage the entire operation.

Now imagine doing that thousands of times.

That's extremely complicated.

Franchising changes the equation.

Instead of one company funding and operating every location, franchisees provide capital and operate restaurants under the company's system.

McDonald's can then scale its brand across a huge network.

The important lesson here isn't simply that McDonald's sells burgers.

It's that McDonald's built a system that allows other people to operate restaurants while McDonald's participates economically through its franchise relationships.

## PART 4 — THE REAL-ESTATE CONNECTION — 3:45–4:50

Now we get to one of the most interesting parts.

Real estate.

Restaurants need locations.

Locations have value.

McDonald's business model can involve the company owning or controlling real estate and collecting rent from franchisees.

This creates another source of economics beyond simply selling food.

So when you see a McDonald's restaurant sitting on a busy road, there can be several layers to the business:

The customer buys food.

The franchisee operates the restaurant.

McDonald's provides the brand and business system.

And depending on the arrangement, McDonald's can receive royalties and rent.

That's a very different picture from simply saying:

"McDonald's sells hamburgers."

## PART 5 — WHY THE MODEL IS POWERFUL — 4:50–5:50

The real lesson is scalability.

A great business doesn't necessarily have to make money in only one way.

McDonald's combines several pieces:

A global brand.

Franchising.

Royalties.

Rent.

Restaurant operations.

And real estate relationships.

Each piece supports the larger system.

And because the company has built a recognizable brand and standardized operating model, the system can be repeated across thousands of locations.

That's the part entrepreneurs should pay attention to.

The product is important.

But the business model surrounding the product can be even more important.

## PART 6 — THE BUSINESS LESSON — 5:50–6:40

Here's the MoneyDecoded lesson.

When you're studying a successful company, don't just ask:

"What does it sell?"

Ask:

"How does it actually make money?"

Who pays?

What do they pay for?

Is the payment recurring?

Who owns the important assets?

Who takes the operating risk?

And can the model scale?

McDonald's is a perfect example of why those questions matter.

Because the burger is what customers see.

The business model is what's underneath.

